+# CTR comparison
+
+Run `python3 analysis_outputs/analyze.py` from the workspace root.
+SQL and returned aggregates are in queries.json; check inputs are in the
+completeness-*, volume-*, and mix-shift JSON files. Check outputs are in
+check-results.json and the supplied runner appends its outputs to ../.ds-checks.log.
+Results and interval assumptions are in results.json.
+
+Before: September 21–27, 2026. After: September 28–October 4, 2026.
+Provisional metric: SUM(clicks) / SUM(searches); no metrics.json or dashboard
+reference is supplied. Binomial intervals require clicks to count searches
+with a click, and independent searches. Neither assumption is verified by
+these daily aggregates. No user/session identifiers are available.
+
+Adjusted change uses platform × country rates and fixes segment weights at
+the arithmetic mean of the two weeks' search shares. The symmetric Kitagawa
+mix contribution plus adjusted change equals the observed change exactly.
+This is a descriptive decomposition, not causal attribution. Version and
+week are perfectly confounded. A randomized concurrent v1/v2 comparison
+would identify the causal effect under appropriate experiment assumptions.
+If v2 itself changes search composition, fixing the mix could remove part
+of its total effect. Other time changes can also offset a ranker effect.
+
+Completeness checks use row counts and search volumes at each week endpoint,
+with trailing-day baselines due to short history. The grid check covers all
+requested dates and segments. These checks cannot verify ingestion against
+an upstream watermark or detect consistent undercounting.
+
+The rollback threshold was requested but is not supplied in the initial task.
+The adjusted interval is not a causal bound and cannot certify v2's safety.
